  "explain": {
   "text": "More equal pieces in one whole means smaller pieces. Each piece is 1000 mm ÷ pieces: 5 give 200 mm, 10 give 100 mm. When the tops match, the bigger bottom is the smaller fraction.[^1][^2]\n\nThe trap is the whole-number habit: 10 is more than 5, so 1/10 looks bigger.[^3] But if a tenth were over 200 mm, ten of them would make more than 2000 mm from one metre.\n\n**Same whole, more pieces, smaller pieces: a tenth of a metre, 100 mm, is shorter than a fifth.**"
  },
  "analogy": {
   "text": "Matching lamps in series on 230 V share it: two get 115 V each, ten get 23 V. More lamps, less each.",
   "breaks": "Only in series: in parallel each lamp sees near enough the full 230 V."
  },
  "practice": {
   "text": "**Rule of thumb:** same whole, same top: bigger bottom, smaller piece.\n\n**Worked example:** 2400 mm of trunking in equal bays. 2400 ÷ 4 = 600 mm; 2400 ÷ 8 = 300 mm. More bays, shorter bays.\n\n**Common mistakes**\n- Thinking 1/10 beats 1/5 because 10 beats 5.\n- Using the rule when the tops differ: 3/10 beats 1/5."
  },
